From a6dd3db4406d3b8ae73eb28cb948f4ebd3a6dbc2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: XEROX710 <151706170+nobody71004@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 03:22:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Keep a television's own glass from hiding its picture under
 an upscaler

Reported from XyberRP: blocks of a television's picture go missing, worse
the more the set is seen from the side. Nothing is in front of it; the
depth test hides the picture behind the set's own glass.

The test compared the screen's depth at the pixel being drawn with the
game's depth in the four texels around it. An upscaler draws that depth
smaller than the picture -- a third of it each way under DLSS/FSR ultra
performance (the reporting client: 853x533 for 2560x1600) -- so those
texels were sampled up to one and a half texels, four and a half pixels,
from the pixel. On a set seen at an angle the glass there is nearer than
the picture at the pixel by the screen's slope times that distance, which
passes the 3 cm tolerance from about 65 degrees, and the picture was hidden
in texel-sized blocks. At full-resolution depth the distance is under a
pixel, which is why the suite, which went to 50 degrees at half
resolution, never saw it.

Each texel is now tested against the screen's depth where the game
sampled it, at the texel's centre: the plane's inverse depth is affine in
the pixel, so that is one dot product per texel. The game samples a texel
where TAA's jitter put it that frame, anywhere in it, so the tolerance also
takes half a texel of the screen's slope -- nothing on a set seen square,
centimetres on one seen nearly edge-on, where a texel's worth of depth is
all the buffer can resolve anyway.

Measured with the suite's ray-traced scenes (the shader's own arithmetic as
C++), with nothing in front of the set, over depth at 1, 2/3, 1/2 and 1/3
of the picture, TAA jitter of 0 to half a texel, 30 to 82 degrees, a 1.16 m
television (glass 0.4 mm proud), a zero-thickness panel and the 100 ft
cinema (glass 1 cm proud) at their distances: up to 75 % of a screen's
pixels hidden or faded before, none after. With a hand, a pillar and a
person in front of the set over the same sweep, the picture shown through
them away from their edges is exactly what it was before (the tolerance
band where something crosses the screen's plane).

Pinned in Open77.ScreenDepth.Tests: the scene's depth can now be sampled
with jitter; nothing in front of a set at 65, 75 and 82 degrees with depth
at a half and a third and jitter 0 to 0.5 shows all of the picture (fails
on main at 75 degrees, half resolution); the cinema the same at 40 m; a
hand and a pillar still hide it at 65 degrees and a third. The shader
compiles with dxc (ps_6_0, vs_6_0, warnings as errors);
ScreenDepthShaderSource.hpp is regenerated from the two files.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_018pk9hktezx1wpdp98cblfp
